---
/** How "Is it wet?" works, in plain words: a section of the About page on every site. */
import { MODELS, RAIN } from '@isithot/core';

const pct = (share: number): string => `${Math.round(share * 100)}%`;
---

<h2>Is it wet?</h2>
<p>
  For rain, we ask {MODELS.length} forecast models how much they expect, hour by hour. A model
  "expects rain" when it forecasts at least {RAIN.day.wet} mm for the day. For a shorter stretch,
  like the rest of an evening, less is enough: {RAIN.hour.wet} mm for each hour that is left. The
  share of models expecting rain is the chance. When {pct(RAIN.likelyFrom)} or more of them do, the
  answer is Yes; from {pct(RAIN.maybeFrom)} it is Maybe. How much rain comes from the middle
  forecast (the median), so one model's downpour cannot make the day wet on its own.
</p>
<p>
  Today means the rest of today, from now until midnight. Rain that has already fallen no longer
  counts, so the answer can change through the day. When most of it is forecast to fall as snow,
  judged by the same rule as "Will it snow?", the answer says so.
</p>
<p>
  This week adds up each model's rain for the rest of today and the next six days, and compares the
  total with the rainfall that is normal there at this time of year, from NASA POWER's long-term
  records.
</p>
